 for sym in required_symbols):\\n            retained.remove(nid)\\n            removed_order.append(nid)\\n    return {\\\"retained\\\": sorted(list(retained)), \\\"removed\\\": removed_order}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to produce a JSON object with key \"implementation\". The value is a Python source code string defining function `solve(case)` that implements a grammar-guided node reduction. Based on the description, we have a tree of nodes with parent references, a list of required symbols. We need to return a result that includes retained IDs and removal order. The problem appears to be similar to Perses reduction: remove nodes that are removable, respecting grammar validity (i.e., all required symbols must be present after removal). The algorithm: traverse nodes in order of depth (deeper first) and within same depth, node ID as tie-break (stable). For each removable node, tentatively remove it (i.e., consider its children being orphaned? Actually, the description says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So a node can be removed only if it has no retained children? Or if it is removable and its removal still keeps required symbols. But also \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we need to check if after removing that node, the set of symbols still includes all required_symbols. However, removing a node means its subtree is gone. So the symbols from that subtree are lost. So we must ensure that all required symbols are still present elsewhere in the tree.\n\nAlso \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we process nodes in descending depth order, breaking ties by node ID (presumably lexicographically or by some order). \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\"\n\n\"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That means we cannot remove a node if it has children that are still in the tree? Actually, \"retained children\" likely means children that are not removed yet. So if we are considering removal, we must check that the node's children are not retained. But since we process deeper first, children are deeper, so they might have been considered earlier. If a child was retained (not removed), then we cannot remove the parent. So we need to track which nodes are retained.\n\nThe output: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So we should return a dict with keys \"retained\" (list of node IDs in sorted order) and \"removed\" (list of node IDs in the order they were removed). Also maybe \"removal_order\"? The description says \"actual removal order\". We'll return \"retained\" and \"removed\". But we need to confirm. The candidate artifact mentions \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" and \"solve(case)\". The input case has nodes list with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. And required_symbols. We need to validate required fields exist.\n\nImplementation plan:\n- Parse case payload: nodes list, required_symbols list.\n- Build a dict mapping id to node info.\n- Validate: all nodes have required fields (id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility). Also require required_symbols list. Check that root exists and parent null.\n- Build children mapping (parent -> list of child ids).\n- Initialize set of retained node IDs: all nodes.\n- Initialize set of required_symbols (from required_symbols). Note: root symbol \"root\" is typically required? The required_symbols includes \"root\" and \"s1\" in examples. So root is forced retained. Also any node with symbol in required_symbols is forced retained? The condition: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So a required symbol node cannot be removed. So we should never remove any node whose symbol is in required_symbols, even if removable? That seems correct. So if a node's symbol is in required_symbols, it must be retained.\n- Also note: \"Never remove a node with retained children.\" So if a node has any child that is still retained, we cannot remove it.\n- We process nodes in order: sort nodes by depth descending, then by node ID stable (e.g., by string order? The examples use ids like \"n1\", \"n2\", \"n3\"... likely lexicographic order is fine, but to be stable, use the exact string order as given? Since IDs may not be numeric, we should sort by depth descending and then by the ID string as a tiebreaker. But the problem says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So we can just sort tuples (-depth, id). That will give deeper first, and within same depth, alphabetical order.\n- For each node in that order:\n    - Skip if node is root (parent null) or if node symbol in required_symbols.\n    - Skip if node is already removed? We'll maintain a set of removed nodes; initially empty. We'll iterate over the sorted list of all nodes, but we only consider nodes that are still retained. However, when we process a node that has been removed earlier? Since we process deeper first, removal of a child might affect parent's ability to be removed later (because parent would lose a child). But order is fixed: we process all nodes in sorted order. If a node is removed, it won't be considered again because it's in the removed set. But when we later consider its parent, we must check if the parent still has retained children. Since the child is removed, that child is not retained, so it's fine. So we need to check current retained children.\n    - Also check \"removable\" field: if node[\"removable\"] is False, skip.\n    - Check: Does the node have any retained children? i.e., any child ID that is still in retained set. If yes, skip.\n    - Check: If we remove this node, will all required symbols still be present? To check, we need to consider that removing the node removes its entire subtree. So we must ensure that for each required symbol, there exists at least one node (including the subtree of other node