ssed; if they were removable and removed, they are already gone. If they were not removed (e.g., required symbol), then they would still be present, but we are now removing the parent, which would also remove them. But the condition \"node with retained children\" prevents removing a node with retained children. So if any descendant is retained, we cannot remove the parent. So it's guaranteed that no retained children exist. So after we remove a node, we don't need to explicitly remove descendants because they are already removed. So we just remove the node itself.\n    - Continue.\n- After processing all nodes, we have retained nodes and removed nodes list (in order of removal). The retained nodes should include all required symbols and root. Return sorted(retained IDs) and the removal order list.\n- The function should return a JSON-serializable object, so a dict with keys \"retained\" and \"removed\" (or \"removal_order\"?). The description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So \"retained\" (sorted list) and \"removed\" (list in removal order). We'll use \"removed\" as key for the order.\n- Also need to validate the case: check that all nodes have required fields. If not, raise ValueError.\n- Also check that required_symbols is a list.\n\nWe'll implement solve(case) accordingly.\n\nNow, we need to produce the JSON object with key \"implementation\" and the Python source as a string. We must escape newlines, backslashes, quotes. Write the code with proper indentation. Use triple-quoted raw string? But JSON requires escaping. We'll produce a multiline string with \\n for newlines and \\\" for quotes. We'll write the code in a compact but readable way.\n\nLet's write the Python code.\n\nWe'll include a main solve function:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # Extract payload\n    payload = case.get(\"payload\", case)  # case might be the payload directly? The fixtures show case has payload key. But we'll handle both.\n    if \"payload\" in case:\n        payload = case[\"payload\"]\n    nodes = payload[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = payload[\"required_symbols\"]\n    # Validation\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list) or not isinstance(required_symbols, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid payload\")\n    # Build maps\n    node_map = {}\n    for node in nodes:\n        # check required fields\n        for key in (\"id\",\"parent\",\"symbol\",\"depth\",\"removable\",\"utility\"):\n            if key not in node:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Missing field {key} in node\")\n        node_map[node[\"id\"]] = node\n    # Build children map\n    children = {nid: [] for nid in node_map}\n    for node in nodes:\n        pid = node[\"parent\"]\n        if pid is not None:\n            if pid not in children:\n                # Or ignore? But should be valid tree\n                continue\n            children[pid].append(node[\"id\"])\n    # Initial retained set (all nodes)\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\n    removed_order = []\n    # Process nodes in depth descending, then id stable\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node[\"id\"]\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        # Check if root or required symbol\n        if node[\"parent\"] is None or node[\"symbol\"] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        # Check removable flag\n        if not node[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        # Check if any retained children\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in children.get(nid, [])):\n            continue\n        # Check if after removal, all required symbols still represented\n        # Compute set of symbols in retained nodes (excluding this node and its subtree)\n        # Since no retained children, subtree is just this node.\n        # But to be safe, we compute all retained symbols except this node's symbol.\n        # However, if there are deeper nodes that are retained? We already checked no retained children, but still could be deeper descendants? Actually, if no retained children, then no descendants retained. So only this node.\n        # So we can just check if required_symbols - set of symbols from other retained nodes is empty.\n        # Simpler: compute symbol counts across retained nodes, then subtract this node's symbol; if any required symbol count goes to 0, reject.\n        # We'll compute symbol counts.\n        symbol_counts = {}\n        for rid in retained:\n            sym = node_map[rid][\"symbol\"]\n            symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\n        # Subtract this node\n        this_sym = node[\"symbol\"]\n        symbol_counts[this_sym] -= 1\n        if symbol_counts[this_sym] == 0:\n            del symbol_counts[this_sym]\n        # Check all required symbols present\n        if all(symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) > 0 for sym in required_symbols):\n            # Remove\n            retained.remove(nid)\n            removed_order.append(nid)\n    # Return result\n    return {\n        \"retained\": sorted(list(retained)),\n        \"removed\": removed_order\n    }\n\nBut there is nuance: The required symbols may be multiple. Also root is implicitly required? The required_symbols includes \"root\". So root is covered.\n\nWe should also consider that after removal, we might have removed a node that had children that were already removed. That is fine.\n\nEdge case: If a node is removable but its symbol is required, we skip it. But what if that symbol is required but the node is the only one with that symbol? Then we cannot remove it anyway. So fine.\n\nNow, we need to output the code string. Use proper escaping.\n\nWe'll produce the JSON object with \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    ...\" etc.\n\nWe must ensure the first character is '{' and last is '}', no extra text.\n\nLet's write the escaped string. We'll generate it programmatically in our mind. But we'll output as JSON.\n\